1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,640
As the saying goes, ‘time waits for no man’.
常言道，“岁月不待人”。

2
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:09,050
Time is always against us, and we just can’t stop it.
时间总在与我们作对，而我们对此束手无策。

3
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:13,910
Maybe that’s why some of us are always running late for appointments.
或许这就是有些人赴约总迟到的原因。

4
00:00:13,910 --> 00:00:23,630
But if timekeeping is not what you’re good at, don’t stress. There might be a good reason for your lack of punctuality.
但如果你不擅长守时，不必焦虑，你缺乏准时性或许是有充分理由的。

5
00:00:23,630 --> 00:00:27,010
People’s attitudes to being on time vary.
人们对待守时的态度各不相同。

6
00:00:27,010 --> 00:00:31,680
Some clock-watch and make sure they’re bang on time for a meeting.
有些人总盯着时钟，确保开会准时到场。

7
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:38,300
It is, after all, rude to be late, and if you can make it on time, why can’t everyone else?
毕竟，迟到是不礼貌的行为，既然你能准时，其他人为什么不能呢？

8
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:48,200
But if, like me, you want to make every second count, you might try to squeeze as much as you can into the time you have available.
但如果你和我一样，想让每一秒都过得有意义，就可能会试图在有限的时间里塞进尽可能多的事情。

9
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:55,700
However, when your schedule doesn’t run to plan, your punctuality inevitably slips.
然而，当你的日程安排未能按计划进行时，你就难免会变得不守时。

10
00:00:55,700 --> 00:01:00,800
People who lack promptness have been described as ‘time benders’.
那些做事拖沓的人被称为“时间拖延者”。

11
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:13,530
Author Grace Pacie told the BBC that “they’re the people who don’t want to be late, but they have a strange resistance to being early, and they don’t allow enough time.”
作家格雷丝·帕西在接受BBC采访时表示：“这类人其实不想迟到，但他们就是莫名抗拒早到，而且预留的出行时间总是不够。”

12
00:01:13,530 --> 00:01:20,880
They assume their journey to an appointment will always go smoothly, and the train will always be on time!
他们想当然地认为，赴约路上一定会一帆风顺，火车也总会准点到站。

13
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:28,210
Perceptions of unpunctual people are almost always negative – even if sometimes wrong.
人们对不守时者的看法几乎都是负面的——即便有时这种看法并不准确。

14
00:01:28,210 --> 00:01:37,820
Writing for the BBC, Laura Clarke says: “Being consistently late might not be your fault. It could be your type.
劳拉·克拉克在为BBC撰写的文章中称：“总是迟到或许不是你的错，可能是性格使然。

15
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:52,020
The punctually-challenged often share personality characteristics, such as optimism, low levels of self-control, anxiety, or a penchant for thrill-seeking, experts say.
专家表示，不守时的人往往有着相似的性格特质，比如乐观、自控力差、容易焦虑或是喜欢寻求刺激。

16
00:01:52,020 --> 00:02:01,260
It is also possible people are late so not to be conspicuous and to avoid the anxious wait for others to turn up.
还有一种可能是，人们迟到是为了避免过于引人注目，同时也不想焦灼地等待他人到来。

17
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:11,135
Maybe us latecomers should make more of an effort and follow the advice I heard to not ‘try’ to be on time but ‘decide’ to be on time.
或许我们这些迟到星人应该再努努力，遵循我听过的一条建议：不要“试着”准时，而是“下定决心”准时。

18
00:02:11,135 --> 00:02:16,630
But I know if a meeting or a deadline really matters, then I’ll be there.
不过我心里清楚，要是某个会议或截止日期真的很重要，我肯定会准时到场。

19
00:02:16,630 --> 00:02:21,440
Otherwise relax, even if others are cursing you!
至于其他情况，就放宽心吧，即便别人在心里咒骂你也没关系！
